{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794817672,"nonce":"hL0s7zFu6qrEjT01nhvJUMaA0Tk=","message":"Phase 3, 16415b92: Cranelift compiles a program that is not bytecode. CraneliftTierContext::for_module takes a ModuleInfo instead of a bytecode pointer, ctx.bytecode() is now fallible (an error for a program with none), and AshCraneliftBackend::compile_air compiles a function that arrives as AIR. The entry signature, call signatures, constant pools and type kinds come from the module; ModuleInfo gains function_name; a program identity replaces the bytecode address in the retier key. What the module cannot answer yet (objects, enums, natives, runtime types) is an error for a program with no bytecode, never a guess. ash_nair_hl/tests/cranelift.rs compiles nAIR's numeric kernels this way and runs them as machine code against Rust reference results, as lowered and after O3: fib, gcd, collatz, a float loop, a primality test, clamp, a boolean chain, no bytecode in the process. Gate before commit: clippy in all three CI configurations, ash_core lib and air tests, the nAIR and agreement tests, the tier parity matrix, osr_retier, tree fusion and the simd and ref tests. Next: objects. Field layout and the runtime type descriptors (hl_type*) are what a nAIR class needs from the module and from an address provider, which is the runtime-owned trait of decision 1.","files":null}]}